# Line endings.
#
# This repo was developed on macOS, where the shell scripts are executable and
# every file is LF. It has since been opened on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`,
# which made git warn about all ~270 files ("LF will be replaced by CRLF") and
# would have rewritten every file's line endings on the next checkout — turning a
# one-line edit into a whole-file diff.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working copy to LF on every platform, so the checked-out bytes
# match the indexed bytes and `git diff` shows only real changes. Windows editors
# handle LF fine; nothing in this project needs CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even if someone changes the rules above — a CRLF
# shebang script fails on Linux/macOS with a confusing "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Source and docs are text. Listed explicitly so a future `binary` rule can't
# accidentally swallow them.
*.py   text
*.html text
*.css  text
*.js   text
*.md   text
*.json text

# CSV data exports are NOT normalized.
#
# The five `data/*.csv` files are machine-generated exports that were committed
# with CRLF bytes, and they are the reference data the university seed script
# reads — the bytes are the artifact, not the source. Letting `* text=auto eol=lf`
# reach them made all five show as modified with ~1,160 changed lines that
# changed no value at all, and committing that would bury real work in noise.
# `-text` means "do not convert"; diffs still work, so a genuine data change is
# still reviewable.
*.csv -text
*.ini  text
*.txt  text
*.mako text

# Binaries: never diff, never convert.
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   text
*.db    binary
*.sqlite binary
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
